ght so if we evaluate the top two  primary system on its own claimed areas  of superiority we have studies that find  that number one when you have more  candidates to choose  people don't really end up actually  voting for the moderates they often  actually get confused about the  ideological differences between the  extreme candidates and the moderate  candidates within the same party and  it's very frequent  it's very frequently happening that the  more extreme members of either the  Republican or Democratic Party are  actually winning so number one that has  not worked it is not moderated election  results in any way  and number two turnout went down in the  first two statewide primaries that were  held in California with the top two  primary rule so by its own stated goals  Pat it hasn't succeeded so if we did a  top two primary for the 2016  presidential election yeah any guesses  as to who the two candidates would be  the two final candidates for the general  uh so how would we so this would be  based on total votes in all primaries if  you like it add all the states together  yet which granted isn't the best way to  do it because they would have run  different races Republicans had like 17  candidates so that diluted some of the  support that other candidates might have  gotten would it but uh Hillary and Ted  Cruz Donald Trump versus Hillary Clinton  oh it would have that would have been it  oh yes but Bernie Sanders wasn't that  far behind Donald Trump so it could have  also easily been Hillary versus Bernie  in the general election fair enough yeah  fair enough and what we've seen at least  in California is that it has not gone I  listen I think that what we need is one  of the many versions of Instant Runoff  voting I know that I talked about  Instant Runoff voting quite a bit it's  also sometimes called ranked choice  voting and people write in and they say  David you know there are systems that  are better than the exact version of  Instant Runoff that